;;; A value parameter its activator binds to a nested fact, read only by the
;;; rule's conclusion, stops the run — M1f **W58**, register row
;;; `W58/parameter-nested-assert` (`corpus/wrong_answers.toml`), filed by
;;; S1f.5.10 (T1f.5.10.1, 2026-11-09) outside its case, `open` and owned by
;;; S1f.5.18, whose W33 it shares a cause with.
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One fact `(p a)`, a rule `act` whose parameter `?y` only its `:assert`
;;; reads, and the activator `(act (f a))`, which binds `?y` to the nested
;;; fact `(f a)`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; An activator binds a rule's parameter to its argument, a nested fact a
;;; value as any other (the user's ruling of 2026-11-08 on S1f.5.5's census,
;;; *"Q3 - as recommended"*, which W33 records): `act` fires on `(p a)` with
;;; `?y = (f a)`, so root holds `(hit (f a))`, and the minimal line is
;;; root's state.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answers ────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The compiler seeds a parameter from its activator and skips a nested
;;; fact (*a nested Fact does not bind*, W33's cause), so `?y` reaches the
;;; conclusion unbound and the run stops at root, exit 1, with `unbound var
;;; ?y in :assert — bindings: {'x': 'a'}`, on every command (`ein solve
;;; FILE` at `2eb483e0`, release and debug alike). The loader cannot refuse
;;; it: a parameter is bound by its activator, and S1f.5.10's check holds a
;;; conclusion's variables to that, so it is the one path that still reaches
;;; the stop after S1f.5.10.

(relation p Node :why "{?1} is a node")
(relation hit Node :why "{?1} is hit")
(p a)
(rule act (?y)
  :match (p ?x)
  :assert (hit ?y)
  :why "{?y} is hit, since {?x} is a p")
(act (f a))
